  "question": {
    "text": "<p>The number of seven digit positive integers formed using the digits <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math>&nbsp;only and sum of the digits equal to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>12</mn></math> is<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mo>.</mo></math></p>"
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "413",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>The given numbers are&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></math>&nbsp;and sum of digits should be&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>12</mn></math>.</p><p>Case&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>:</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><menclose notation=\"bottom\"><mn>4</mn></menclose><mo>&#160;</mo><menclose notation=\"bottom\"><mn>3</mn></menclose><mo>&#160;</mo><menclose notation=\"bottom\"><mn>1</mn></menclose><mo>&#160;</mo><menclose notation=\"bottom\"><mn>1</mn></menclose><mo>&#160;</mo><menclose notation=\"bottom\"><mn>1</mn></menclose><mo>&#160;</mo><menclose notation=\"bottom\"><mn>1</mn></menclose><mo>&#160;</mo><menclose notation=\"bottom\"><mn>1</mn></menclose></math>&nbsp;,Here the sum of the digits is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>12</mn></math>.</p><p>The number of ways arranging them is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>42</mn></math>.</p><p>Case&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>:</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><menclose notation=\"bottom\"><mn>4</mn></menclose><mo>&#160;</mo><menclose notation=\"bottom\"><mn>2</mn></menclose><mo>&#160;</mo><menclose notation=\"bottom\"><mn>2</mn></menclose><mo>&#160;</mo><menclose notation=\"bottom\"><mn>1</mn></menclose><mo>&#160;</mo><menclose notation=\"bottom\"><mn>1</mn></menclose><mo>&#160;</mo><menclose notation=\"bottom\"><mn>1</mn></menclose><mo>&#160;</mo><menclose notation=\"bottom\"><mn>1</mn></menclose></math>.</p><p>The number of ways arranging them is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>!</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>105</mn></math></p><p>Case&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>:</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><menclose notation=\"bottom\"><mn>3</mn></menclose><mo>&#160;</mo><menclose notation=\"bottom\"><mn>2</mn></menclose><mo>&#160;</mo><menclose notation=\"bottom\"><mn>2</mn></menclose><mo>&#160;</mo><menclose notation=\"bottom\"><mn>2</mn></menclose><mo>&#160;</mo><menclose notation=\"bottom\"><mn>1</mn></menclose><mo>&#160;</mo><menclose notation=\"bottom\"><mn>1</mn></menclose><mo>&#160;</mo><menclose notation=\"bottom\"><mn>1</mn></menclose></math>.</p><p>The number of ways arranging them is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>!</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>140</mn></math></p><p>Case&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>:</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><menclose notation=\"bottom\"><mn>3</mn></menclose><mo>&#160;</mo><menclose notation=\"bottom\"><mn>3</mn></menclose><mo>&#160;</mo><menclose notation=\"bottom\"><mn>2</mn></menclose><mo>&#160;</mo><menclose notation=\"bottom\"><mn>1</mn></menclose><mo>&#160;</mo><menclose notation=\"bottom\"><mn>1</mn></menclose><mo>&#160;</mo><menclose notation=\"bottom\"><mn>1</mn></menclose><mo>&#160;</mo><menclose notation=\"bottom\"><mn>1</mn></menclose></math>.</p><p>The number of ways arranging them is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>!</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>105</mn></math></p><p>Case&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>:</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><menclose notation=\"bottom\"><mn>2</mn></menclose><mo>&#160;</mo><menclose notation=\"bottom\"><mn>2</mn></menclose><mo>&#160;</mo><menclose notation=\"bottom\"><mn>2</mn></menclose><mo>&#160;</mo><menclose notation=\"bottom\"><mn>2</mn></menclose><mo>&#160;</mo><menclose notation=\"bottom\"><mn>2</mn></menclose><mo>&#160;</mo><menclose notation=\"bottom\"><mn>1</mn></menclose><mo>&#160;</mo><menclose notation=\"bottom\"><mn>1</mn></menclose></math></p><p>The number of ways arranging them is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>!</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>21</mn></math></p><p>The total number of ways are&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>42</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>105</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>140</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>105</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>21</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>413</mn></math></p><p>Therefore, the required answer is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>413</mn></math>.</p></blockquote>"
